\begin_layout Subsection
Brownian motion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Brownian motion is defined either
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
as a stochastic process 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(W_{t}\right)_{t\ge0}$
\end_inset

 satisfying three properties:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $W_{0}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 is normally distributed with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

;
 and all increments (of the same length) 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

 share that same distribution;
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Later I will write 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

 to mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{0,t}$
\end_inset

,
 so just 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

,
 but written in the increment style to stress that we are interested in increments of a particular length.
 By stationarity,
 expected values then are the same for increments not started at zero,
 too.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For 
\begin_inset Formula $s<t$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{s}\perp\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 independent increments
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
via a limiting distribution on the space of continuous functions 
\begin_inset Formula $C([0,1],\mathbb{R}^{n})$
\end_inset

 of the distributions of random walks on uniform grid 
\begin_inset Formula $\{t_{i}\coloneqq i/n\}_{i=0}^{n}$
\end_inset

 with jump 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\xi_{i}\right)_{i=0}^{n}$
\end_inset

 be a standard random walk where the jump 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}=\xi_{i+1}-\xi_{i}$
\end_inset

 is either 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

 with equal probability 
\begin_inset Formula $0.5$
\end_inset

.
 We think of time here as flowing discretely in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{t_{i}\coloneqq i/n\}_{i=0}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now conceptually
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In N.V.
 Krylov's book 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Introduction to the theory of random processes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 this is stated and proven rigorously (
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

 is first extended from the discrete points 
\begin_inset Formula $\{t_{i}\}_{i}$
\end_inset

 continuously to the whole interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 the distribution of the random vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\xi_{1}\ldots\xi_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 converges to a distribution on the space of continuous functions 
\begin_inset Formula $C([0,1],\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is trivial
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Krylov's book here also proves that this definition makes sense.
 That is not as trivial.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 now to define Brownian motion on the probability space 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\coloneqq C([0,1],\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

 with that limit distribution as a measure by just projecting each sample (trajectory,
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]\to\Omega$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{for }\omega\in\Omega:\quad W_{t}(\omega)\coloneqq\omega_{t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The former is the definition useful for applications/proving properties of the brownian motion,
 while the latter gives an idea how it came to be.
 As long as we believe in those two definitions and trust that they're equivalent,
 we're good to proceed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two free-hanging postulates in these definitions that are important to understand first,
 though:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If we believe that 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a process of stationary and independent increments,
 what is left to motivate is
\end_layout

