\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence to construct a brownian motion with finite but non-vanishing variation,
 the random walks it is built from must use a jump 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{k}^{(n)}=\pm\alpha_{n}$
\end_inset

 of order 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

.
 We simply pick
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We could pick any constant and would get the limit of the variation scaled accordingly;
 a madman might choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{n}=\frac{\sin n}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset

 but then their 
\begin_inset Formula $\Var\xi_{t}^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 would not converge to a single value.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{n}=1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

,
 which gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Var\xi_{t}^{(n)}=t^{(n)}\cdot\cancel{\left(\alpha_{n}\sqrt{n}\right)^{2}}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}t.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is how we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

 for the random walk jumps 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{(n)}$
\end_inset

,
 and variance 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (deviation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{t}$
\end_inset

) for the Gaussian increments 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Stochastic integral for simple functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dP}{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dW}{\text{d}W}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\d}{\mathrm{d}}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a Lebesgue-Stieltjes integral style we can integrate simple predictable functions against the brownian motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remember that the Lebesgue-Stieltjes integral against a function (of bounded variation) 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{X}f\d g
\]

\end_inset

works similarly to the standard Lebesgue integral but instead of using a 
\emph on
measure 
\emph default
for the floor increments (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(x-y)$
\end_inset

) when infinitesimally multiplying the integrated function,
 it uses the provided function 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

's increment,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta g=g(x)-g(y)$
\end_inset

,
 to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

measure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A simple process is a process taking finitely many values that we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1},\ldots,f_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 it is expressible as a finite sum
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f^{\omega}(t)=\sum_{n}f_{n}^{\omega}\textbf{1}_{\left[t_{n},t_{n+1}\right]}(t)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we define the stochastic integral against the brownian motion 
\begin_inset Formula $\dW_{s}$
\end_inset

 of simple predictable functions as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{0}^{t}f(s)\dW_{s}\coloneqq\sum_{n}f_{n}\Delta W_{n}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Square of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

output is expected to be the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

input
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are a range of facts that I summarize under this section's title.
 The chaos of the material is vastly reduced by viewing many results as an instance of this pattern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Of 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 itself
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Discretely
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second view of Brownian motion above also tells us that considering one jump of the random walk,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{time: } & t_{i}\to t_{i+1}\\
\text{space: } & \xi_{i}\to\xi_{i+1}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

With the choice 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t_{i}=1/n$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}$
\end_inset

 is either 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus the square 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is most definitely 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{n}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\xi_{i}^{2}=\Delta t_{t}
\]

\end_inset

 —
 the square of the output's increment equals the input increment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Continuously
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the limiting distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

 is definitely does not square to just 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(t-s\right)$
\end_inset

,
 but it is interesting that its mean 
\emph on
does
\emph default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}\left(\Delta W_{t}^{2}\right)=t.
\]

\end_inset

 We see this from the property charaterising the distribution of the (non-squared) increment 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left(\Delta W_{t}^{2}\right) & \equiv\int_{\Omega}\Delta W_{t}^{2}\dP=\int_{\mathbb{R}}y^{2}\dP_{Z}\\
 & =\int_{\mathbb{R}}y^{2}\Phi(y)\d y\overset{!}{=}t\int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi(y)\d y=t
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overset{!}{=}$
\end_inset

 we used 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(y)=$
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 denoting the variance of the normal variable,
 i.e.
 here just 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

;
 also omitting integral signs)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
y^{2}e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y & ={\color{blue}-2\sigma^{2}y}e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y^{{\color{blue}2}}/{\color{blue}-2\sigma^{2}}\\
 & =-y\d e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\\
 & =-ye^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}+e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Of the Itô integral for simple functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Above we computed the expected square of the raw increments 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 In a martingale-transformation fashion we can consider the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

transformed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 increments 
\begin_inset Formula $f\Delta W$
\end_inset

 and their integral's expected square:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\int_{0}^{t}f(s)\dW_{s}\right)^{2}\right] & \equiv\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\sum\nolimits_{n}f_{n}\Delta W_{n}\right)^{2}\right]\\
 & \overset{\text{lin}}{=}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n}f_{n}^{2}\Delta W_{n}^{2}\right]+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n\neq m}f_{n}f_{m}\Delta W_{n}\Delta W_{m}\right]\\
 & \overset{\perp}{=}\sum_{n}\mathbb{E}\left[f_{n}^{2}\right]\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta W_{n}^{2}\right]}_{\Delta t}+\cancel{2\sum_{n<m}\mathbb{E}\left[f_{n}f_{m}\Delta W_{n}\right]\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta W_{m}\right]}_{0}}\\
 & =\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n}f_{n}^{2}\Delta t\right]\\
 & \equiv\mathop{\mathbb{E}}_{\omega}\left[\int_{0}^{t}f_{\omega}^{2}(s)\d s\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

which I roughly interpret as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Box ovalbox
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "100col%"
special "none"
height "1in"
height_special "totalheight"
thickness "0.4pt"
separation "10pt"
shadowsize "4pt"
framecolor "foreground"
backgroundcolor "none"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The expected 
\series bold
square
\series default
 of the modified (by 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}$
\end_inset

) output (of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

) equals the 
\series bold
linear
\series default
 integral of the output's modification's square.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a way,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 collapses inside the integral to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

,
 while the 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{2}$
\end_inset

 multiple remains as a squre.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This peculiar fact gives the Ito integral the name 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Ito's Isometry
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (for the very special case of simple functions).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Itô of a deterministic process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is a bit of a stretch,
 but for a deterministic processes the stochastic integral can also be seen to exhibit this output-is-square-of-input pattern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{s}$
\end_inset

 is deterministic,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is just a function 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta:\left[0,T\right]\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\theta_{s}\dW_{s}$
\end_inset

 is normal with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\theta_{s}^{2}\d s$
\end_inset

.
 That is near-obvious for 
\emph on
simple
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta(t)=\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 over intervals 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in[t_{i},t_{i+1}]$
\end_inset

,
 because then the Itô integral 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{0}^{t}\theta_{s}\dW_{s}=\sum_{i}\theta_{i}\Delta W_{i}
\]

\end_inset

is a weighted sum of the independent normal variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 and that is again normal.
 The variance is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Var\int_{0}^{t}\theta_{s}\dW_{s} & =\sum_{i}\Var\theta_{i}\Delta W_{i}\\
 & \overset{\perp}{=}\sum_{i}\theta_{i}^{2}\Var\Delta W_{i}\overset{W}{=}\sum_{i}\theta_{i}^{2}\Delta t_{i}\\
 & =\int_{0}^{t}\theta_{s}^{\boldsymbol{2}}\d s
\end{align*}

\end_inset

the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

square-weighted
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 input.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=1$
\end_inset

,
 this recovers 
\begin_inset Formula $\Var W_{t}=t$
\end_inset

,
 while for other non-uniform weights,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is re-weighted with the square of the integrand to 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{t}\theta_{s}^{2}\d s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Itô's formula
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ito's formula is just an application of Tailor's formula for a stochastic version of the Fundamental theorem of calculus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tailor's formula
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta f(x)\equiv f(x+\Delta x)-f(x)=f'(x)\Delta x+\frac{1}{2}f''(x)\Delta x^{2}+\text{rest}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $x=W_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the principle 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\Delta W\right)^{2}\sim\Delta t$
\end_inset

 applied to the second order term reads
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Delta f(W_{t}) & =f'(W_{t})\Delta W+\frac{1}{2}f''(W_{t})\Delta W^{2}+\text{rest}\\
 & \sim\frac{1}{2}f''(W_{t})\Delta t+f'(W_{t})\Delta W+\text{rest}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Summing that at the points of a partition 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{0},\ldots,t_{n}$
\end_inset

 and taking a limit this turns out to be,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
df(W)=\frac{1}{2}f''(W)dt+f'(W)dW.
\]

\end_inset

One day I want to write this down rigorously,
 but no time today for it.
\end_layout

